          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What kind of physical activity is described for people with type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "For adults with type 2 diabetes, the guidance in the \"Physical activity for type 2 diabetes\" summary is:\n\n- **Aerobic activity:** At least 150 minutes per week of moderate-to-vigorous activity, spread over at least three days. No more than two days in a row should pass without activity.\n- **Resistance training:** Strength work on two to three non-consecutive days a week.\n\nThe summary says spreading activity through the week matters. Its insulin-sensitivity benefit lasts roughly 24 to 72 hours after a session, so activity squeezed into one weekend leaves most of the week without that benefit. It also explains that muscle contraction moves glucose into cells without needing insulin, which is why activity helps lower blood glucose.\n\nThe diabetes summary does not define \"moderate\" or \"vigorous.\" A separate summary, \"Physical activity for high blood pressure,\" describes moderate activity as breathing harder while still able to talk but not sing, such as brisk walking or cycling on the flat. Vigorous activity leaves you unable to say more than a few words without pausing.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not cover how to start if you are inactive, safe exercise for people with complications, or how activity interacts with glucose-lowering medicines. Please discuss those with your clinician before making big changes.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
